# The release gate hashes the checked-out working tree.
#
# scripts/hash-release-inputs.mjs reads every tracked file and digests its bytes
# into release_inputs_sha256, which binds the rendered-fidelity sign-off to the
# code it was reviewed against. With no .gitattributes that digest depended on
# whichever core.autocrlf the checkout happened to use: this repository stores LF
# in the index, so a Windows checkout with autocrlf=true produces CRLF working
# files and a different digest than the same commit checked out with
# autocrlf=input. The sign-off would then be rejected on the release runner for a
# reason that has nothing to do with what was reviewed.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the working tree byte-identical to the index on every
# machine, so the digest is a property of the commit rather than of the client
# that fetched it. Nothing in the repository changes: the index is already LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted. text=auto already detects
# these, but the release digest and the pinned SHA-256 checks in
# scripts/verify-assets.mjs make silent corruption expensive enough to be
# explicit about it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.gguf binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.zip binary
*.traineddata binary
